      prompt: "The following is an excerpt from a short story.\n\n\"My grandmother gave me her recipe box the year her hands got too unsteady to cook, and told me only, 'The good one is near the back, behind the ones that don't matter.' I found it eventually — a stained, handwritten card for a pound cake, the only recipe in the entire box with no measurements at all, just a list of ingredients and the single instruction: 'Mix until it feels right. You will know.'\n\nI made it four times before I understood what she meant, each attempt a little closer, though I couldn't have told you, any of those four times, exactly what 'closer' meant either. The fifth time, standing at my own counter years after she'd stopped being able to stand at hers, I stopped measuring entirely halfway through, my hands simply moving the way I'd watched hers move a hundred times as a child, and the cake came out right — not close to right, actually right — before I'd consciously decided anything about how much of what to add.\n\nI called her that night to tell her. She was quiet for a moment, then said, 'That's not really about cake, you know.' I said I knew. I hadn't known, not fully, until she said it out loud, though some part of me must have already understood, standing at that counter, hands moving on their own, that I was being handed something considerably larger and less measurable than a recipe, in a box designed to look like it only ever held recipes.\"\n\nWrite an essay that analyzes how the writer uses literary techniques to develop the meaning of the passage.",
